    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d764">

                <pb facs="1044" n="924" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg924"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/2785: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Technical Board</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stevens</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-10-27T15:00:00+08:26"><hi rendition="#italic">October 27,
                                1922—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 8:16 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>In regard to the instructions forwarded to Japanese representative Technical
                    Board by the Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d764-f1"/> points 1 and 2 all right. Point 3 all right so far as
                    minutes of the <pb facs="1045" n="925" xml:id="pg925"/> proceedings are
                    concerned but as to other papers no, too indefinite, impossible. Point 4 seems
                    wrong as it was always understood expenditure Allied funds was to be a charge
                    against future Russian Government. I hear that Inter-Allied Railway Committee
                    held its final meeting on October 24th and dissolved itself and subcommittees. I
                    am awaiting instructions from the Department as to the Technical Board. Copy
                    furnished Tokio.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stevens</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d764-f1" n="13" xml:id="d764-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram no. 172, Oct. 17, from the Ambassador in Japan, <ref target="#pg920"
                        >p. 920</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
